      "id": 1,
      "claim": "Oscar Wilde was convicted in 1895 in the United Kingdom under the Criminal Law Amendment Act 1885 for \"gross indecency\" involving consensual homosexual acts.",
      "evidence": "Historical records of Wilde's trial and sentencing (e.g., The Trial of Oscar Wilde, 1895; Criminal Law Amendment Act 1885).",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "In the United States, the Supreme Court decision Lawrence v. Texas (2003) held that the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment protects private, consensual adult sexual conduct from criminal prosecution.",
      "evidence": "Lawrence v. Texas, 539 U.S. 558 (2003).",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The holding in Lawrence invalidated all statutes that criminalize private consensual adult same‑sex conduct, rendering such statutes unconstitutional under the Fourteenth Amendment.",
      "evidence": "Reasoning in Lawrence and its progeny (e.g., Obergefell v. Hodges, 2015) that the liberty interest includes intimate conduct.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "A law analogous to the UK statute used to convict Wilde would be unconstitutional if applied in the United States today.",
      "evidence": "Logical application of Step 3: any law criminalizing the protected conduct is invalid.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Under U.S. constitutional jurisprudence, a law that violates the Due Process or Equal Protection Clauses is legally unfair; the Supreme Court describes such violations as contrary to fundamental fairness (see Lawrence’s discussion of liberty and fairness).",
      "evidence": "Lawrence v. Texas, supra, ¶ 31 (court emphasizes that criminalizing private consensual conduct demeans a class of persons and is fundamentally unfair); general due‑process doctrine that fairness is measured by constitutional compliance.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [4]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "No",
